      (22,41,"`View` lets the same code walk rows or columns. For `Axis::Y` a line is a grid row and you walk along x; for `Axis::X` a line is a column and you walk along y. `occupied` asks whether a cell has at least `min_column` points. `position` is the centre coordinate of a line, `edge` the coordinate of a cell boundary along it."),
      (43,73,"`find_runs`. For every line, walk the cells. A busy cell either extends the open run or, if the gap since the last busy cell is longer than `max_gap`, closes it and starts a new one. `keep` stores a run only if it is at least half of `min_length` long: shorter runs are the 1&ndash;2-cell cross-sections that perpendicular walls leave in every line, and they would glue lines together."),
